#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# time_constrained

#% text
# `time_constrained(e, t)` evaluates `e` for at most `t` seconds.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is the value of `e` if the evaluation ends within `t` seconds; otherwise the evaluation is stopped and the value is `failure(limit, time)`.

#% item
# `time_constrained(e, t, fail)` evaluates `fail` instead of giving the failure.

#% item
# `e` and `fail` are held: they are evaluated by `time_constrained` itself. The limit `t` is evaluated and must be a non-negative number of seconds; with another value the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# The limit stops long library computations too (they poll the interrupt), and the session goes on normally after it. The time of a stopped evaluation is a little more than `t` (the next poll).

#% item
# With nested limits the shorter one decides: an inner limit that expires gives the inner failure or `fail`, and the outer evaluation goes on; an outer limit that expires stops everything inside.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
time_constrained(pause(10), 0.5, "timed out")
#% output
#= "timed out"

#% cell
time_constrained(2 + 3, 1)
#% output
#= 5

#% cell
time_constrained(pause(10), 0.2)
#% output
#! failure(limit, time)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Library computations that take long are stopped: the digits of `pi`, a long table, a numerical integral at high precision:

#% cell
time_constrained(n(pi, 10^6), 0.2, "stopped")
#% output
#= "stopped"

#% cell
time_constrained(table(k^2, k, range(1, 10^8)), 0.2)
#% output
#! failure(limit, time)

#% cell
time_constrained(n_integrate(sin(1/t)*t, [t, 0, 1], working_precision: 40), 0.2)
#% output
#! failure(limit, time)

#% text
# A computation that ends in time gives its value, and the result of an evaluation of the third argument is any expression:

#% cell
time_constrained(integrate(exp(-x^2)*sin(x)/x, [x, 0, inf]), 1)
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi*erf(1/2)

#% cell
time_constrained(pause(10), 0.3, a + b)
#% output
#= a + b

#% text
# The shorter of nested limits decides:

#% cell
time_constrained(time_constrained(pause(10), 0.3, "inner"), 5)
#% output
#= "inner"

#% cell
time_constrained(time_constrained(pause(10), 5), 0.3)
#% output
#! failure(limit, time)

#% text
# Each call in a `map` gets its own limit:

#% cell
map(t => time_constrained(pause(10), t, t), [0.1, 0.2])
#% output
#= [0.1, 0.2]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The time used is close to the limit; the first element of `timing` is the seconds spent:

#% cell
first(timing(time_constrained(pause(5), 0.1))) < 0.5
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The expression is held: with the limit 0 it is not evaluated at all, and the assignment in it is not made:

#% cell
v = 1; time_constrained(v = 5, 0); v
#% output
#! failure(limit, time)
#= 1

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A limit of 0 stops even a trivial evaluation, since no time is allowed:

#% cell
time_constrained(1 + 1, 0)
#% output
#! failure(limit, time)

#% text
# A limit that is not a non-negative number (a negative number, a symbol, a string, `inf`) leaves the call unevaluated; for no limit just evaluate the expression:

#% cell
time_constrained(1 + 1, -1)
#% output
#= time_constrained(1 + 1, -1)

#% cell
time_constrained(1 + 1, inf)
#% output
#= time_constrained(1 + 1, inf)

#% text
# A failure in the third argument is the result:

#% cell
time_constrained(pause(10), 0.3, 1/0)
#% output
#! failure(division_by_zero)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `memory_constrained`, `pause`, `timing`
